.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-GET-ROLE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-get-role \- show one role and what it grants
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam get-role
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints one role's JSON representation: its name, description, ERN, and
the permissions it holds.
.SH WHICH ROLE OF THAT NAME
Your account's own role of that name, and the built-in one only if you
have none \- resolved in that order.
.PP
That is the order a grant resolves in, which is why this command resolves
the same way rather than reporting both. A grant names a role by name,
and the question worth answering is what a grant of that name would
actually use. Two answers would leave the reader to work that out.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-role (1)
refuses to create a role whose name is a built-in one, so in practice the
two cannot collide; this order is what makes that refusal necessary
rather than the other way round.
.SH READING A BUILT-IN ROLE
The permissions of a built-in role are computed from the permission
vocabulary rather than stored, so what this prints is current as of this
build \-
.I reader
lists the read actions of every module there is now, including any added
since the installation was deployed. That is the one thing a role of your
own cannot do.
.PP
.I account\-administrator
prints as
.IR *:* ,
which is every action of every bindable module and still not
installation administration: it does not reach
.I emm
or
.IR emd .
See
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Role name. A built-in name also works. Required.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Installation administrators \- members of the
.I administrator
user group. See
.B ADMINISTRATOR PRIVILEGES
in
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The role was found; its JSON representation is printed.
.TP
.B 1
No name given, no role of that name in your account and no built-in one
either, or the caller is not an administrator. If the failure was an
expired or invalid session (\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored credentials file
is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# What the built-in operator role actually covers
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam get-role \e
    --name operator

# One of your own
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam get-role \e
    --name order-service
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-roles (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-update-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-permissions (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-grant-role (1)
